 "exercises": [
  {
   "id": "a1",
   "source_id": "Which shows that the pressure can be expressed solely terms of the partition function:",
   "kind": "exercise",
   "bloom": "Apply",
   "place": {
    "at": "end"
   },
   "cite": "ideal-gas-law",
   "prompt": "<p>One mole of an ideal gas fills 22.4 L at 273.15 K. With $\\ln Q = N\\ln q - \\ln N!$ and $q = (2\\pi m/h^2\\beta)^{3/2}V$, use $\\langle P \\rangle = kT(\\partial \\ln Q/\\partial V)_{N,\\beta}$ to find its pressure in pascals.</p>",
   "answer": {
    "type": "number",
    "value": 101400.0,
    "unit": "Pa",
    "generated_by": "ai",
    "hint": "Only $N\\ln V$ in $\\ln Q$ changes with $V$.",
    "solution": "<p>Only the term $N\\ln V$ of $\\ln Q$ depends on the volume, so $(\\partial \\ln Q/\\partial V)_{N,\\beta} = N/V$ and $\\langle P \\rangle = NkT/V$. With $N = 6.022 \\times 10^{23}$, $k = 1.381 \\times 10^{-23}$ J/K, $T = 273.15$ K and $V = 0.0224$ m³: $\\langle P \\rangle = \\dfrac{(6.022 \\times 10^{23})(1.381 \\times 10^{-23}\\ \\text{J/K})(273.15\\ \\text{K})}{0.0224\\ \\text{m}^3} = 1.014 \\times 10^5$ Pa, about one atmosphere.</p>"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "a2",
   "source_id": "Only one term in the ln $Q$ depends on $V$.",
   "kind": "exercise",
   "bloom": "Analyze",
   "place": {
    "at": "end"
   },
   "cite": "ideal-gas-law",
   "prompt": "<p>Suppose each of $N$ molecules cannot enter a small volume $b$ held by the others, so that the partition function becomes $Q = \\dfrac{1}{N!}\\left[\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi m}{h^2\\beta}\\right)^{3/2}(V - Nb)\\right]^N$. Use $\\langle P \\rangle = kT(\\partial \\ln Q/\\partial V)_{N,\\beta}$ to find the equation of state.</p>",
   "answer": {
    "type": "open",
    "generated_by": "ai",
    "hint": "Take the logarithm first; only $N\\ln(V - Nb)$ depends on $V$.",
    "solution": "<p>$\\ln Q = \\dfrac{3N}{2}\\ln\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi m}{h^2\\beta}\\right) + N\\ln(V - Nb) - \\ln N!$. Only the middle term depends on $V$, so $\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial \\ln Q}{\\partial V}\\right)_{N,\\beta} = \\dfrac{N}{V - Nb}$ and $\\langle P \\rangle = \\dfrac{NkT}{V - Nb}$. The molecules move as in an ideal gas, but in the free volume $V - Nb$ only, which is the repulsive part of the van der Waals equation (Section 16.2).</p>"
   }
  }
 ],
